// @test: run
// @level: L2
// @tier: 0
// @tags: name-resolution, builtins, shadowing
// @timeout: 20000
// @expected-stdout-begin
// mine 99
// builtin 2
// mine-again 99
// @expected-stdout-end
// @description: A unit's own `fn min` outranks the ambient builtin.
//
//   T1120. `docs/architecture/name-resolution.md` §3.2 ranks a
//   module-local declaration 4 and an ambient name 6, so a local
//   declaration must win. It did not: `compile_call` asked
//   `try_compile_builtin` first (expressions.rs:6203) and returned,
//   while the test that consults `unit_declared_fns` sat 528 lines
//   below in the same function, unreachable for any name the builtin
//   table carries. `min(1, 2)` printed 1 — the builtin's answer — and
//   the user's body never ran. Silently, with no diagnostic.
//
//   BOTH POLES LIVE IN THIS ONE FILE, deliberately:
//
//     `min` IS declared here      -> must print the USER's 99
//     `max` is NOT declared here  -> must print the BUILTIN's 2
//
//   The second is not decoration. A "fix" that simply stopped
//   consulting the builtin table would satisfy the first line and
//   break every program in the language; without the `max` line the
//   spec could not tell that repair from this one.
//
//   The third line calls `min` again after `max` has forced a builtin
//   lookup in between, because the resolution state is per-call and a
//   fix that memoised the wrong answer would still pass two lines.
//
//   99 is chosen because it is not the answer for ANY argument order:
//   min(1,2), max(1,2), and either argument alone are all in {1, 2},
//   so a wrong path cannot coincidentally produce it.

fn min(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
    // Deliberately not a minimum. The point is whose body runs.
    99
}

fn main() {
    print(f"mine {min(1, 2)}");
    print(f"builtin {max(1, 2)}");
    print(f"mine-again {min(5, 7)}");
}
